---
/**
 * Site header with a full-screen mobile navigation overlay.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY THIS IS BUILT ON <details> AND NOT A JAVASCRIPT TOGGLE
 *
 * Brief §2.1: the site must work with JavaScript disabled — "all navigation
 * functional". A conventional hamburger wired to a click handler fails that
 * outright: with JS off the button does nothing and the entire navigation
 * becomes unreachable on mobile.
 *
 * Brief §4 already prescribes the answer for expandable content: use the
 * native <details>/<summary>. It toggles with zero JavaScript, is keyboard
 * operable, and is announced as a disclosure control by screen readers.
 *
 * WHY THE LINKS ARE A SIBLING OF <details> RATHER THAN ITS CHILDREN
 *
 * The obvious structure — links nested inside the <details> — cannot be made
 * to display inline on desktop. Browsers hide a closed <details>'s content via
 * `content-visibility: hidden` on an internal shadow-DOM slot, which author
 * CSS cannot override. (Chrome 131+ exposes `::details-content` for exactly
 * this, but Safari and Firefox do not, so relying on it would break desktop
 * navigation in those browsers.) This was verified empirically, not assumed:
 * the nested version rendered the links with correct computed styles but zero
 * layout height, and they were unreachable at every desktop width.
 *
 * So <details> holds only the toggle and acts as a pure state container, and
 * `:has()` drives the overlay on its sibling <nav>. The links are ordinary
 * markup that is always rendered — nothing depends on how a browser chooses
 * to render collapsed <details> content.
 *
 * FOCUS CONTAINMENT WITHOUT A JAVASCRIPT FOCUS TRAP
 *
 * WCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.11 (Focus Not Obscured) is satisfied by setting <main> and
 * <footer> to visibility:hidden while the overlay is open, which removes
 * everything behind it from the tab order and the accessibility tree. A
 * full-screen menu hides that content visually anyway, so this matches what
 * the user sees.
 *
 * visibility:hidden, not display:none — deliberately. Every NAV link is now a
 * same-page `/#id` anchor rather than a separate route, so tapping one while
 * the overlay is open is a same-document fragment navigation: the browser
 * tries to scroll to the target immediately, and it never retries later.
 * display:none would remove <main>'s layout box at that exact moment, so the
 * target section has nowhere to scroll to and the scroll silently fails —
 * closing the overlay afterwards does not fix it, the page is left wherever
 * it was. visibility:hidden keeps the layout box (so the scroll still lands
 * correctly) while still excluding the content from the tab order.
 *
 * That fragment scroll happens immediately, while the overlay is still open —
 * <details> only ever closes via its own <summary>, an anchor click can't
 * close it. `.primary-nav` is position:fixed, so it keeps covering the
 * viewport through that scroll regardless of where the document ends up. But
 * `.site-header` (which holds the close control) was only position:relative
 * at the time, so the fragment scroll carried it — and the only way to close
 * the overlay — out of view along with it, with nothing on screen to
 * indicate that had happened. Confirmed empirically with Playwright (JS
 * disabled): the scroll itself worked, but the close button became
 * unreachable immediately afterwards.
 *
 * `.site-header` is now sticky on every mobile viewport, not just while the
 * overlay is open — a deliberate UX choice so the toggle is always one tap
 * away on a long page, not just a fix for the bug above. It still resolves
 * that bug as a side effect: the close control (and the section a scroll
 * just landed on) stay reachable no matter where the page has scrolled to.
 *
 * ONE PIECE OF JAVASCRIPT — THE FIRST IN THIS CODEBASE
 *
 * Tapping a NAV link while the overlay is open scrolls correctly (fixed
 * above) but does not close the overlay — `<details>` only ever closes via
 * its own <summary>, and there is no zero-JS way to close it as a side
 * effect of an unrelated link click. A `:target`-based CSS alternative was
 * tried and rejected: `:target` is a persistent match on the current URL
 * fragment, not a one-time "just navigated" event, so a rule that force-hides
 * the overlay while any section is `:target`ed would also block it from ever
 * reopening again for the rest of the visit, once any anchor had been
 * visited once. There is no CSS-only way to express "just navigated" as
 * distinct from "currently navigated."
 *
 * The script below closes the overlay when a NAV link is clicked. It is
 * strictly progressive enhancement, not a dependency: with JavaScript
 * disabled, the link still works and the overlay simply stays open until the
 * user taps the toggle once more — the pre-existing, fully-functional
 * behavior everywhere above this comment. With it enabled, the overlay
 * closes immediately, matching what the scroll already did underneath it.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import { SITE, NAV, FOOTER_NAV } from '../consts.js';
import t from '../i18n/en.js';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';
---

<header class="site-header">
  <div class="wrap site-header__inner">
    <a class="site-header__brand" href={withBase('/')}>
      <span class="site-header__name"><span class="site-header__name-mark">D</span>TLM</span>
    </a>

    <!--
      State container only. Its <summary> is the hamburger when closed and the
      ✕ when open — one control, one focus stop, no state to synchronise.
    -->
    <details class="nav-toggle-wrap" id="site-nav">
      <summary class="nav-toggle" aria-controls="primary-nav">
        <span class="nav-toggle__icon nav-toggle__icon--menu">
          <span class="nav-toggle__bars" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="visually-hidden">Open menu</span>
        </span>
        <span class="nav-toggle__icon nav-toggle__icon--close">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path
              d="M5 5 L19 19 M19 5 L5 19"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.6"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              fill="none"></path>
          </svg>
          <span class="visually-hidden">Close menu</span>
        </span>
      </summary>
    </details>

    <nav id="primary-nav" class="primary-nav" aria-label={t['nav.primary']}>
      <ul class="primary-nav__list">
        {
          NAV.map((item) => (
            <li>
              <a href={withBase(item.href)}>{t[item.key]}</a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <!--
        Secondary links appear inside the overlay on small screens only. On
        desktop they live in the footer, so they are hidden here rather than
        announced twice.
      -->
      <ul class="primary-nav__secondary">
        {FOOTER_NAV.map((item) => <li><a href={withBase(item.href)}>{t[item.key]}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>

<script>
  // Progressive enhancement only — see "ONE PIECE OF JAVASCRIPT" above.
  // Closes the mobile overlay when a nav link is tapped. Without this,
  // the link still works; the overlay just needs a second tap to close.
  const nav = document.getElementById('site-nav');
  const primaryNav = document.getElementById('primary-nav');
  if (nav && primaryNav) {
    primaryNav.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      if (event.target.closest('a')) nav.open = false;
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .site-header {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--paper);
    position: relative;
    z-index: 50;
  }

  /*
   * Sticky on mobile so the toggle (and the nav it reveals) is always one
   * tap away, without scrolling back to the top of a long page. Desktop
   * doesn't need this — the nav is already fully visible inline.
   *
   * Not gated behind @supports selector(html:has(body)) — position:sticky
   * doesn't depend on :has() at all, so browsers without it still get a
   * persistent header even though they fall back to wrapped inline links
   * instead of the overlay (see the MOBILE — full-screen overlay comment
   * below).
   */
  @media (max-width: 59.999rem) {
    .site-header {
      position: sticky;
      top: 0;
    }
  }

  .site-header__inner {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-s) var(--space-l);
    padding-block: var(--space-m);
  }

  .site-header__brand {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    display: block;
    /* Stays above the overlay so it remains visible and clickable. */
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
  }

  .site-header__name {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .site-header__name-mark {
    color: var(--brand-mark);
  }

  /* ===================================================================
     Toggle control
     =================================================================== */

  .nav-toggle-wrap {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
  }

  .nav-toggle {
    list-style: none; /* removes the native disclosure triangle */
    cursor: pointer;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* Comfortably above the 24×24px WCAG 2.2 target minimum (§7). */
    width: 44px;
    height: 44px;
    margin-inline-end: -10px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .nav-toggle::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .nav-toggle__icon {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  /* Hamburger bars drawn in CSS — no icon request. */
  .nav-toggle__bars,
  .nav-toggle__bars::before,
  .nav-toggle__bars::after {
    display: block;
    width: 22px;
    height: 1.5px;
    background: currentColor;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  .nav-toggle__bars {
    position: relative;
  }

  .nav-toggle__bars::before,
  .nav-toggle__bars::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
  }

  .nav-toggle__bars::before {
    top: -7px;
  }
  .nav-toggle__bars::after {
    top: 7px;
  }

  /* Swap hamburger ⇄ ✕ from the open state alone. */
  .nav-toggle__icon--close {
    display: none;
  }

  #site-nav[open] .nav-toggle__icon--menu {
    display: none;
  }

  #site-nav[open] .nav-toggle__icon--close {
    display: flex;
  }

  /* ===================================================================
     Navigation — desktop default (inline), overlay applied below
     =================================================================== */

  .primary-nav__list,
  .primary-nav__secondary {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .primary-nav__list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-s) var(--space-m);
  }

  .primary-nav__list a {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    padding-block: var(--space-3xs);
    border-block-end: 1px solid transparent;
  }

  .primary-nav__list a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
    border-block-end-color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* Secondary links belong to the overlay only. */
  .primary-nav__secondary {
    display: none;
  }

  /* Desktop needs no toggle. */
  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .nav-toggle-wrap {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* ===================================================================
     MOBILE — full-screen overlay
     ===================================================================
     Wrapped in @supports: if a browser cannot do :has(), the overlay can
     never be opened, so the navigation must NOT be hidden. Such browsers
     fall back to the links simply wrapping onto a second line — less tidy,
     but navigation stays reachable. Requirement §2.1 is never traded for
     appearance.
     =================================================================== */

  @supports selector(html:has(body)) {
    @media (max-width: 59.999rem) {
      .primary-nav {
        display: none;
      }

      /*
       * `top` clears the header's own height, not `inset: 0`, deliberately.
       * primary-nav is a *child* of .site-header (sibling of the brand and
       * toggle, both siblings of the header's own background) — a child's
       * painted content always layers above its own ancestor's background,
       * regardless of z-index, because z-index only orders positioned
       * *siblings* against each other. Giving primary-nav z-index:1 stacks
       * it correctly below the brand/toggle (z-index:2, real siblings), but
       * there is no z-index that makes a descendant paint *behind* its own
       * ancestor's background — that relationship isn't something z-index
       * can express. With `inset: 0`, primary-nav's box reached all the way
       * to the top of the viewport, so once its own scrollable content (not
       * the page — see the sticky-header comment above for that) was
       * scrolled, links slid up and rendered on top of the sticky header's
       * background, covering the brand name instead of being covered by it.
       * Starting the box at `top` instead means the overlay's own
       * `overflow-y: auto` clips that content at the header's lower edge —
       * scrolled links disappear into the header-height gap rather than
       * ever reaching the header's screen area, so there is nothing left to
       * out-stack in the first place.
       */
      .site-header:has(#site-nav[open]) .primary-nav {
        display: block;
        position: fixed;
        top: calc(var(--space-2xl) + var(--space-s));
        right: 0;
        bottom: 0;
        left: 0;
        z-index: 1;
        background: var(--paper);
        padding: var(--space-l) var(--gutter) var(--space-xl);
        overflow-y: auto;
        overscroll-behavior: contain;
        animation: nav-in 180ms ease;
      }

      .primary-nav__list {
        display: grid;
        gap: 0;
      }

      .primary-nav__list a {
        display: block;
        font-size: var(--step-2);
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
        text-transform: none;
        color: var(--ink);
        padding-block: var(--space-m);
        border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule);
      }

      .primary-nav__secondary {
        display: grid;
        gap: var(--space-2xs);
        margin-block-start: var(--space-xl);
      }

      .primary-nav__secondary a {
        display: inline-block;
        color: var(--ink-muted);
        font-size: var(--step--1);
        padding-block: var(--space-3xs);
      }
    }
  }

  @keyframes nav-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  /*
   * Global, because these rules target <main> and <footer>, which are siblings
   * of the header and therefore outside this component's scoped styles.
   *
   * While the overlay is open on small screens:
   *   - page content is removed from the tab order (SC 2.4.11), and
   *   - the page behind cannot scroll.
   *
   * visibility:hidden rather than display:none — see the FOCUS CONTAINMENT
   * comment at the top of this file for why: a fragment-navigation nav tap
   * needs <main> to still have a layout box to scroll to.
   *
   * Scoped to #site-nav so project popovers are unaffected.
   */
  @supports selector(html:has(body)) {
    @media (max-width: 59.999rem) {
      body:has(#site-nav[open]) {
        overflow: hidden;
      }

      body:has(#site-nav[open]) > main,
      body:has(#site-nav[open]) > footer {
        visibility: hidden;
      }
    }
  }
</style>
